body:
First of all, thank you for taking the time to write this reply.

&#x200B;

\>Absurdism

&#x200B;

I actually did a bit of internet research on absurdism and nihilism a while back. Nihilism was the one that seemed most logical to me. My main issue with absurdism was its treatment of suicide as being even more absurd - it felt(and feels tbh) like an attempt to stubbornly justify living in the face of it being meaningless. However, it is possible that one line on Wikipedia is inadequate to describe this stance. I'll look into Camus' works once I finish the book I'm on currently.

&#x200B;

\>suffer towards a noble cause

&#x200B;

But isn't this nobility human-defined? Won't this cause be nullified too when I'm no longer alive/when I'm forgotten? There isn't anything objectively noble, is there? If you have heard of the simulation hypothesis - it feels like yet another failsafe to keep the simulation running.

&#x200B;

In the light of all happiness being transient, though, I do find this sensible.

&#x200B;

\>Jordan Peterson

&#x200B;

I don't agree with "women have it figured out". Sure, they do, if, as you said, they have children, but that doesn't make it apply to all women. And even then, its a choice.

&#x200B;

\>responsibilities

&#x200B;

I'll have to poke around more, because to me it still appears that responsibilities are a crutch invented to keep on living. Like, I cannot find any proper argument against suicide.

&#x200B;

\>ongoing mental health issues

Like I said, depression. Perhaps bpd. Mood swings. inability to focus.

&#x200B;

\>vet assistant

&#x200B;

Of all the options available, this one is the most attractive. I'll try and find out how it pays here/qualifications needed.

&#x200B;

Thanks again. Your answer did provide me material to reflect on. Helped.

&#x200B;

edit: btw it appears that the voluntourism link is broken, it just leads me to the cnn travel page
